<BaseLayout title="An Inverted Pyramid, a Waymo and a City of Vaults — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Four days by car from Los Angeles to Phoenix in March 2025: Tempe Town Lake at night, the ASU campus with Wangcai, the Desert Botanical Garden, a Waymo at a red light, and Arcosanti's concrete vaults north of the city.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · March 2025</p>
  <h1>An Inverted Pyramid, a Waymo and a City of Vaults</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Four days in the Phoenix area: Tempe, Papago Park and Scottsdale, and a morning at Arcosanti.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · March 20–23, 2025</strong><br />
    Lunch in Arcadia → I-10 through the Coachella Valley → the Colorado River at Blythe → the Stack in Phoenix →
    Tempe Town Lake → downtown Tempe and the ASU campus → the Desert Botanical Garden → Old Town Scottsdale →
    downtown Phoenix → north on I-17 to Arcosanti → back on I-10 to the Coachella Valley → home<br />
    Two states: California and Arizona ·
    one upside-down city hall · a few Waymos · one calico cat · one wall of bells ·
    and Wangcai, two months into life with us
  </div>

  <p>
    This was a four-day spring trip by car: drive to Phoenix, spend two days walking around Tempe, Phoenix
    and Scottsdale, and on the way home, stop at Arcosanti, the experimental concrete town in the desert north of the
    city. It was also a trip with <strong>Wangcai</strong>, our cream-colored dog, who had
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">come home in January 2025</a>, two months earlier. She came along the whole way, and
    she shows up in a good number of the photos below.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything is put together from the camera roll: what was photographed, where, and
    at what minute. My own phone has a few dozen photos from these four days. My wife's has a lot more, especially of
    Wangcai, so many of the pictures below are hers. Where neither of us took a photo, I don't say much.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Across the desert to Tempe</h2>
  <p>
    The trip started on Thursday, March 20, with lunch at <strong>Arcadia Garden Cafe</strong> in Arcadia, in the San Gabriel
    Valley, a little after 11 in the morning. Then it was I-10 east. By 3 pm we were in the Coachella Valley, and
    Wangcai was sitting up in the back seat in her harness, watching the desert go by.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-car.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a harness with a pink seat-belt tether, sitting in the back seat of a car and looking out the side window at the desert." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on I-10 in the Coachella Valley, 3:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 4:52 my phone took two short videos as we crossed the Colorado River from Blythe into Ehrenberg, which is the
    California–Arizona line on I-10. Arizona stays on standard time all year, so in March its clocks match
    California's and nothing changes at the border. The next video, at 6:38, is from the <strong>Stack</strong>, the
    big interchange where I-10 meets I-17 west of downtown Phoenix.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0320.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0320.webp'} alt="Map of Day 1: from Arcadia east on I-10 through the Coachella Valley, across the Colorado River at the Arizona line, to the Stack in Phoenix, then to dinner in Tempe and Tempe Town Lake." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: from the San Gabriel Valley to Tempe on I-10.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We stayed three nights at the <strong>DoubleTree by Hilton Phoenix–Tempe</strong>. We got there around
    sunset. At 6:50 my wife took a photo of a small butte with a white "T" on its side, black against an orange
    and pink sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 't-butte-sunset.webp'} alt="A small butte with a white letter T on its side, in silhouette against an orange and pink sunset, with tree branches framing the top and parked cars below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset in Tempe, 6:50 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was late, a little after 9, at a Japanese restaurant on Scottsdale Road: a plate of nigiri (tuna, salmon,
    tamago, eel and white fish), skewers, rice and miso soup, and a pan of udon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sushi.webp'} alt="A black rectangular plate with a row of nigiri: tuna, salmon, white fish, tamago and eel, with a cup of tea beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Nigiri, 9:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After dinner we went down to <strong>Tempe Town Lake</strong>, a lake on the Salt River just north of downtown
    Tempe. At 9:40 the office buildings on the far side were lit up in white, blue and red, and the lights ran across
    the still water in long stripes. A path runs along the north shore, and we walked a short way on it. That was
    the end of the first day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'town-lake.webp'} alt="Tempe Town Lake at night: lit office towers on the far shore reflected in long colored stripes on dark water, with a concrete embankment along the right edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tempe Town Lake from the north path, 9:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: Downtown Tempe and the ASU campus</h2>
  <p>
    Friday was a walking day. The first photo is from 1:19 in the afternoon, at <strong>ASU Gammage</strong>, the
    round, pink auditorium at the south end of the Arizona State campus, behind a row of tall palms. Arizona State's
    main campus is in Tempe, and the city and the university run into each other. Most of the day went back and
    forth between them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'gammage.webp'} alt="A round, pink auditorium with a ring of tall arches, seen across a lawn between tall palm trees under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>ASU Gammage, 1:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By 1:54 we were at the <strong>Tempe Municipal Building</strong>, the city hall. It is a pyramid of dark glass
    and steel set upside down on its point, so each floor is wider than the one below it. Under it, the Council Chambers entrance is half sunk into
    the ground. Wangcai sniffed the gravel bed in front of the sign.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'municipal-building.webp'} alt="An upside-down pyramid of dark glass and steel over a sunken plaza and a stone sign reading TEMPE MUNICIPAL BUILDING, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The inverted pyramid, 1:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-municipal.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a pink leash sniffing a gravel bed in front of a stone sign reading TEMPE MUNICIPAL." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, inspecting city hall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Nearby is <strong>Hayden Butte</strong>, the rocky hill locally called "A" Mountain
    for the big gold letter A painted on its side. Then we walked south along Mill Avenue, past a wall of new
    apartment towers, back toward campus. At 2:19 we were among the arts buildings at the south edge of campus:
    blocky masses of pink-brown stucco with very few windows, above a base of red brick arches.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'asu-arts.webp'} alt="Blocky pinkish-brown stucco buildings with almost no windows rising above a base of red brick arches and green shrubs, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arts buildings on the south side of campus, 2:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Wangcai got a break on the lawns there. In a short video from 2:47 my wife filmed her lying on her
    back in the grass under a tree, with her pink leash trailing toward the camera. At
    3:18 we sat down on the patio of the <strong>Memorial Union</strong>, the student union in the middle of
    campus, and she sat on the patio and looked straight into the camera.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-lawn.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog lying on her back in bright green grass under the trunks of a tree, with a pink leash running toward the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On the lawn, 2:47 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-patio.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog with tall ears sitting on a stone patio between metal tables and chairs, looking into the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>At the Memorial Union, 3:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Memorial Union's ground floor faces a patio with a big "Sun Devils" sign over the food court, and at 3:51 we
    passed a rack of maroon Sun Devils jackets set out on the sidewalk. Around 4:30 we
    stopped at <strong>Premium Matcha Cafe</strong> just east of campus. The photo is of a swirl of purple soft
    serve, on a green base with a strawberry and a dark scoop of something next to it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'purple-soft-serve.webp'} alt="A tall swirl of purple soft serve in a white paper bowl on a green base, with a strawberry and a dark purple scoop beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Purple soft serve, 4:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the last hour of daylight both of our phones were busy again. At 4:56 we were
    on University Drive, where a bridge with the ASU logo on it carries a building over the street and the light-rail
    tracks. Just past it stood a tall tower covered in a diagonal lattice. Then, at 5:13, we reached the
    oldest part of campus: <strong>Old Main</strong>, a red-brick building with steep gables and a deep porch,
    behind a long, low sign that says ARIZONA STATE UNIVERSITY. The low sun turned the brick almost orange.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'asu-bridge.webp'} alt="A wide street with light-rail tracks passing under a bridge with the ASU logo, next to a building with a patterned beige facade, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bridge over University Drive, 4:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'old-main.webp'} alt="Old Main, a red-brick building with steep red roofs and a porch, behind a low ARIZONA STATE UNIVERSITY sign, framed by palm trunks and a tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Old Main, 5:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From there we walked back north toward Mill Avenue, and at 5:28 we came out under "A" Mountain again. The low sun lit up the brown rock and the gold A, next to the long glass wall of the Tempe
    Transportation Center. The last photos of the day, just after 6, are from Tempe Marketplace, a shopping center to
    the east.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'a-mountain.webp'} alt="A rocky brown hill with a gold letter A painted on its side, lit by low sun, beside the long glass and steel facade of a transit building, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"A" Mountain, 5:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0321.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0321.webp'} alt="Map of the Day 2 walk in Tempe: ASU Gammage, the Tempe Municipal Building, the arts buildings, the Memorial Union, the soft-serve stop, the bridge over University Drive, Old Main and A Mountain." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: a long walk between downtown Tempe and the ASU campus.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Papago Park, Scottsdale and downtown Phoenix</h2>
  <p>
    Saturday began at the <strong>Desert Botanical Garden</strong> in Papago Park, between Tempe and Phoenix. On the
    way, at 10:40, we pulled up at a red light behind a <strong>Waymo</strong>, a white Jaguar with a sensor dome
    on the roof. Two minutes later, on Galvin Parkway, there were two of them in front of us, heading toward
    the same red rocks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0322.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0322.webp'} alt="Map of Day 3: the Waymo near Papago Park, the Desert Botanical Garden, lunch and the rabbit sculpture in Old Town Scottsdale, the Arizona Center in downtown Phoenix, and dinner at Tacos Los Vales in west Phoenix." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: Papago Park, Scottsdale, and west across Phoenix for dinner.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waymo-papago.webp'} alt="The back of a white Waymo Jaguar with a sensor dome on the roof, stopped at a red light, with the red Papago Buttes on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Behind a Waymo, with the Papago Buttes ahead, 10:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The garden sits at the foot of the <strong>Papago Buttes</strong>, rounded humps of red sandstone that show up
    behind many of the photos from the morning. Near the entrance, a field of hundreds of small painted cloth
    squares had been set out on stakes in rows, around a tall tower made of more of them. Further in, among the
    saguaros, there were large sculptures in gold-colored metal, cut into lattices of geometric patterns.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'garden-flags.webp'} alt="Rows of small painted cloth squares on stakes filling a sandy field, around a tall tower covered in more of them, with a red butte behind and visitors walking among them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The field of painted squares, 11:09 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lattice-diamond.webp'} alt="A tall diamond-shaped sculpture of gold-colored metal lattice on a round stone platform, with saguaros behind it under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A lattice diamond among the saguaros, 11:19 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    One of them was a tall diamond on a round stone platform. Another was a walk-in pavilion that threw a lace of
    shadows over the wooden floor and over Wangcai.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-lattice.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog standing inside a pavilion of gold metal lattice, with a pattern of light and shadow falling across her coat and the wooden floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai inside the lattice pavilion, 11:54 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We were in the garden until about 12:20, and the gift shop is in my camera roll too, with shelves of chili crisp,
    botanical drinks and cactus snacks. By 1 we were in Old Town Scottsdale, at a domed rotunda in the Main Street
    Arts District. Lunch was at <strong>Arcadia Farms Cafe</strong> at 1:36: a salad with strawberries, chicken and
    almonds, and iced tea. After lunch, at 2:14, I photographed a tall, white, geometric rabbit sculpture outside a
    restaurant.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lunch-scottsdale.webp'} alt="A white bowl of salad with strawberries, chicken and sliced almonds on a marble table, with an iced tea and water glasses around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch at Arcadia Farms Cafe, 1:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The camera rolls are quiet for most of the afternoon. Both of us photographed the same drink that day: a carton
    of prickly-pear cactus water. By 5:35 we were in downtown Phoenix, at the <strong>Arizona Center</strong>, whose big silver
    letters stand on a base covered in pink glitter. Dinner, just before 8, was at
    <strong>Tacos Los Vales</strong> in west Phoenix: a tray of tacos with grilled onions and a roasted pepper,
    and a bowl of soup on the side.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arizona-center.webp'} alt="Large silver letters spelling ARIZONA CENTER on a pink glitter base, in front of a modern building of glass and pale concrete." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Arizona Center, downtown Phoenix, 5:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tacos.webp'} alt="Overhead view of a wooden table with paper-lined trays of tacos topped with onions and a roasted pepper, a foil-wrapped item, and a bowl of soup." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tacos Los Vales, 7:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Day 4: Arcosanti</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday was the drive home, with one stop on the way. We left Phoenix at about
    8:30. At 8:37, on I-10 past Sky Harbor, a plane came in low over the freeway on its way to land. Then we turned
    north on I-17, the Black Canyon Freeway, and climbed out of the valley through hills covered in saguaros. By 9:20
    we were up on the open grassland north of Black Canyon City, under a heavy gray sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i17-sky.webp'} alt="A wide, flat golden grassland beside a highway under a heavy, lumpy gray cloud ceiling with a band of light near the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grassland and clouds on I-17, 9:20 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 9:33 we turned off the highway, and by 9:34 we were at <strong>Arcosanti</strong>. The sign at the start
    of the walkway says "Experience Arcosanti · The Urban Laboratory · Since 1970". It is the experimental town that
    the architect Paolo Soleri started here, on the edge of a canyon. What stands there is a cluster of concrete
    buildings at the edge of the mesa: blocky towers with big round windows, a dome of glass panels, and stairs and
    walkways running between them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-sign.webp'} alt="A sign reading Experience Arcosanti, The Urban Laboratory, beside a concrete walkway that leads into the desert toward low concrete buildings, under heavy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The way in, 9:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-skyline.webp'} alt="Gray concrete buildings with flat roofs, a glass dome and round windows at the edge of the mesa, with desert plants in front and a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The buildings at the edge of the mesa, 9:35 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then there is the <strong>apse</strong>, a huge concrete quarter-sphere open to the front, like
    half of a dome cut in two. Its ceiling is cast in a grid of panels with round red medallions, and under it are
    shelves and work tables. We stood in front of it at 9:53, with an olive tree hanging over the corner of the
    frame.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-apse.webp'} alt="A giant concrete half-dome open at the front, its ceiling patterned with panels and round red medallions, sheltering shelves and a long curved wooden counter, under olive branches." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The apse, 9:53 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    There are bells hanging all over Arcosanti. At 10:10, from a terrace on the canyon
    side, my wife photographed a dark bell hanging against the view: the canyon below, a long cliff of dark
    rock, and flat tablelands going off into the distance under the clouds. A few minutes later a calico cat sat
    in the mulch under an olive tree.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-bell.webp'} alt="A dark bronze bell hanging from a curled hook in front of a wide view of a canyon, a cliff of dark rock and flat tablelands under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bell over the canyon, 10:10 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-cat.webp'} alt="A calico cat with white, black and orange patches sitting in brown mulch under an olive tree, next to a black hose." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Arcosanti cat, 10:16 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The other big structures are the <strong>vaults</strong>: two huge concrete arches, one behind the other,
    painted on their undersides in bands of ochre and red, with a wide paved terrace beneath. Looking through them,
    you see a row of dark cypresses and olive trees, and beyond that, the sky. Below the terrace, the
    buildings step down the canyon wall, with tall cypresses rising next to them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-vaults.webp'} alt="Two great concrete arches, one inside the other, painted underneath in ochre and red bands, over a paved terrace, under a bright blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The vaults, 10:30 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-cypress.webp'} alt="Looking down a canyon slope at tall thin cypresses, canvas shades and terraces, with a wide valley of grass and scrub and distant mesas beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cypresses down the canyon side, 10:48 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Indoors, in a long gallery, dozens of bells, in bronze and ceramic, hang in rows on chains
    in front of a painted wall. My wife's last photo there was at 10:48.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arcosanti-bells-wall.webp'} alt="Rows of bronze and ceramic bells of many shapes hanging on long chains in front of a wall painted with large shapes in red, yellow and green, in a long gallery." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A wall of bells, 10:47 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0323.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0323.webp'} alt="Map of Day 4: from I-10 near Sky Harbor north on I-17 past the grassland to Arcosanti, then west to the Coachella Valley in California." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: north to Arcosanti, then home.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    After that it was the long drive home. The next photos are from 3:40 in the afternoon, back in California in the
    Coachella Valley, where we stopped for Korean barbecue at about 4. Then we drove the rest of the way back to Los Angeles.
  </p>
  <p>
    Looking back through the photos, the trip comes down to a handful of buildings: a city hall standing on its
    point, a round pink auditorium among palm trees, an old red-brick hall, and a town of concrete half-domes on
    the edge of a canyon. In between, Wangcai rolled in the grass and sat for her picture on a lot of different
    floors.
  </p>
